Subject: [PATCH] [mlir] Walk and replace sub-elements from an explicit stack

`AttrTypeWalker` and the attribute and type replacers recurse once per
level of nesting: the walker through `walkSubElements` and the walk
function's callback, the replacers through `replaceSubElements` and
`replace` on every sub-element. The native stack they need grows with how
deeply an attribute or type nests, so walking the symbol uses of an
operation whose attribute is an array nested a few thousand deep
overflows a thread with a small stack.

Both now keep their place in an explicit stack, one frame per element
whose sub-elements are being visited, and visit the same elements in the
same order: an element enters the walker's visited set before its
sub-elements, its walk functions run before or after them as the order
says, a skip passes over them and an interrupt ends the walk of every
element it is under, recorded as each one's result in a post-order walk
as before. The replacers look each element up in their cache first, run
the replacement functions, replace the sub-elements of the replacement in
order until one fails, and record the result in the cache after theirs;
the cyclic replacer's entries are resolved in the reverse order of their
lookups, as its cache requires, and each is kept where it is made, since
an unresolved entry may not be copied. `replaceBase` and the replacers'
`cachedReplaceImpl` give way to `replaceWithCache` and a cache lookup and
resolution on each replacer; the public interface is unchanged.

A unit test walks and replaces an array attribute nested 10,000 deep on a
thread whose stack a frame per level would overflow.
